# Where each vector came from, in terms a machine can check.
#
# The README beside this file says the vectors are the tenant texts "byte for
# byte from its tag". That was true when each was made and nothing verified it
# afterwards: a vector edited by hand, or copied from a working tree rather
# than from the tag, would pass every suite and quietly turn the upgrade test
# into a test of today's text against today's build -- the thing being checked
# and the thing doing the checking coming from the same place. Recording the
# TAG NAME alone does not fix that, because a tag is a movable ref and "the
# repository" does not have one state; what is checkable is the commit the tag
# resolved to when the vector was made.
#
# `tools/lint-provenance.sh`, a gate phase, regenerates every file below from
# the commit named here and compares it byte for byte, so a drifted vector
# fails instead of passing. It also requires that every directory beside this
# file has a section, so a vector added without a record is a failure and not
# a silence.
#
# Format: [<directory>] opens a section; `key = value` lines follow; a line
# whose first non-blank character is '#' is a comment.
#
#   kind    `tag` -- the files are <prefix>/<path> at <commit>.
#   repo    the repository, as <host>/<owner>/<name>. Recorded because a
#           commit means nothing without saying whose it is (coop, 2026-09-12:
#           cite refs, not repositories).
#   ref     the tag the vector was cut from. Checked when this clone has it:
#           if refs/tags/<ref> resolves to any other commit, the tag moved
#           after the vector was made, which is louder than a byte mismatch.
#   commit  what that ref resolved to, in full. This is the record; the tag
#           name is the human's half of it.
#   prefix  the path the vector's files sit under at that commit.
#   made    the day the vector was cut, UTC.

